## 3. Training recipe (identical to exp_01's control; the only permitted variation is the tier)

`XRIR_DATA_PATH=/home/yixunhu/data_cache/AcousticRooms PYTHONHASHSEED=0 OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=<gpu> /home/yixunhu/miniconda3/envs/xRIR/bin/python train_xRIR_backbone.py --backbone {simple|cylindrical} --save-dir ckpt/exp05/<tier>_<backbone>/attempt_<ts> --num-shot 8 --max-len 9600 --lr 1e-3 --weight-decay 1e-4 --decay-epochs 3 --lr-gamma 0.1 --epochs 12 --batch-size 32 --accum-steps 2 --num-workers 12 --seed 0 --tf32 --log-interval 50 --save-every 0 --epoch-ckpt-every 1 --vit-dim <dim> --vit-depth <depth> --vit-heads <heads> --vit-mlp-dim <mlp_dim>` — 296 334 samples / epoch, 9 261 micro-batches, 4 631 updates / epoch, 55 572 updates total; the epoch-12 checkpoint is confirmatory for every arm (exp_01's M pair at epoch 12 likewise); no `best.pth` or test-selected epoch enters a verdict. No resume for reportable runs (checkpoints do not carry sampler/RNG state; an interrupted attempt is preserved as `_ABORTED_` and restarted).

New trainer flags `--vit-dim/--vit-depth/--vit-heads/--vit-mlp-dim` (defaults = M = current behaviour; `args.json` records them) are the only code change to the trainer. If exp_04's `--yaw-aug` flags have landed by then, they stay at 0 here (no augmentation in this experiment).

Timing basis: the M control's measured runtime was 28.2 h for 111 132 micro-batches, i.e. ≈ 0.91 s per micro-batch including test epochs and saves (the 1.4 s/iteration figure in CLAUDE.md is an early estimate; 1.4 × 9 261 × 12 would be 43 h). The ViT runs 2 + K = 10 times per sample, so encoder FLOPs dominate: expect S ≈ 12–16 h and L ≈ 40–55 h; the §7 probes replace these guesses before launch, with a ≤ 60 h gate per arm. Cumulative ceiling: 1.5 × the probe projection per arm over all attempts; exceeding it requires renewed authorisation. Four runs ≈ 110–140 GPU-hours; on two A6000s about 3 days, scheduled around exp_04's training (GPU 0, ≈ 30 h from its launch) — coordinate GPU ownership with the exp_04 session before launching.

## 4. Evaluation protocol (declared; reuse exp_04's tooling when merged, otherwise exp_03's)

- Unseen split, all 6 337 queries, K = 8 (primary) and K = 1 (secondary), at k = 0 only (`--yaw-cols 0 --acoustic-cols 0 --e-acoustic-cols --decomposition-batches 0`), batch 16 canonical, TF32 off, no test-time augmentation. **Evaluator:** `eval_yaw_rotation.py` builds only the M configuration and loads strictly, and it is a member of exp_03's pinned closure — it is NOT edited. exp_05 adds its own entry point `tools/exp05_eval.py` that imports the evaluator's functions unchanged, builds the registered tier from the checkpoint's `args.json` (`vit_dim/depth/heads/mlp_dim`, refusing a checkpoint whose recorded tier differs from the requested one or whose state-dict shapes do not match), loads strictly, records the tier configuration and parameter counts in both output metas, and otherwise reproduces `eval_yaw_rotation.py`'s outputs for the M tier (test: the M control on a 32-query subset at seed 42 through both entry points gives identical query/index order, per-sample values, validity masks and aggregate metrics; only an explicit metadata allowlist — `elapsed_min`, the added tier/provenance fields — may differ).
- **Five evaluation seeds {42, 43, 44, 45, 46}**: reference manifests `ckpt/yaw_aug/reference_manifest_k{8,1}_seed{s}.json` (built 2026-09-12 by exp_04; hashes in `ckpt/yaw_aug/reference_manifests_index.json`) with `--gl-seed s`; all six arms evaluated under every seed → 6 arms × 2 K × 5 seeds = 60 runs (≈ 10 min at K = 8, ≈ 6 min at K = 1: ≈ 8 h of GPU), the M pair's runs reused from exp_04 only under the provenance rule below.
- **Provenance (mandatory, exp_04-equivalent):** every evaluation run has a pre-run evaluation manifest (checkpoint path + sha256, tier configuration, epoch, training `args.json` sha256, evaluator/entry-point/producer source-closure digests at the reviewed exp_05 commit, dataset identity, K, reference-manifest path/hash/seed, GL seed, batch/canonical/TF32, grids, command, environment) written into an exclusively created run directory, and a launcher-owned completion sidecar (closed-log and output digests) written after the child exits; exp_04's `tools/provenance.py` / launchers are reused if they have landed and been reviewed, otherwise exp_05 implements the same contract (§6). exp_01's M evaluations from exp_04 are reused only if their manifests pass exp_05's producer checks (same evaluator closure, same manifests/seeds, same K/grids/precision); otherwise the M pair is re-evaluated (budgeted).
- Yaw robustness per tier (secondary, K = 8, seed 42 only, condition P at k ∈ {0, 32, 64, 448, 480}) for all six arms — the two M arms are re-run at seed 42 under this protocol so the tiers are comparable (exp_03's seed-0 sweeps are historical context only); descriptive, to see whether the exp_03 finding (no robustness gain for cylindrical at M) holds at S and L.

## 5. Pre-registered hypotheses and decision rules

Statistics as exp_03/exp_04: per query, the five per-seed errors are averaged (plain means; a query enters a cell only if finite for all seeds in both arms of the cell); paired per-query differences bootstrapped over queries (20 000 resamples, bootstrap seed 0; query-level primary, this fixed split) with the room-cluster bootstrap (17 rooms) secondary; two-seed convergence gate (max endpoint movement ≤ 10 % of the seed-0 width, else raise `n_boot`). Each hypothesis is a separate family; no omnibus rule.

- **H1 (dominance of the curve; K = 8, EDT and C50 error, epoch 12):** for every tier t ∈ {S, M, L}, δ_t = (mean error_cyl,t − mean error_simple,t) / mean error_simple,t (recomputed inside each paired resample). Family m = 6 (3 tiers × 2 metrics), two-sided adjusted intervals [Q_{0.05/12}, Q_{1−0.05/12}], bootstrap seeds 0/1 with the convergence gate. Verdict per metric: "dominance supported on <metric> at the three tested tiers" iff the adjusted upper bound of δ_t is below 0 at all three tiers; "partial" if at some; "not supported" if none — stated separately for EDT and C50, never as an unqualified "dominance", and never extrapolated between the tested capacities. T60 descriptive. The same table at K = 1 as a separate secondary family, **reported in full alongside K = 8** (all cells, intervals, cohorts and verdicts), with the analysis stating explicitly where the K = 1 conclusions differ from the K = 8 conclusions (exp_03's K = 1 follow-up showed the reversal at M; the curve tells whether it is tier-dependent). H2 is likewise computed and reported at K = 1 as a secondary four-cell family with the same rules.
- **H2 (fixed-target reading; experiment 3): one four-cell family** = {Cyl-S vs Base-M, Cyl-M vs Base-L} × {EDT, C50} at K = 8. The target of a cell is the baseline arm's five-seed mean of the metric **computed on that cell's registered paired cohort** (queries finite for all five seeds in both arms of the cell) — the same cohort on which ρ = (mean error_cyl − mean error_base)/mean error_base is recomputed inside each paired resample (bootstrap seeds 0 and 1). The "reaches the target" claim and any parameter-ratio statement are scoped to that cohort; the baseline's own-cohort curve mean is shown separately and labelled, and the producer reports both together with the cohort sizes (a producer test uses the reviewer's counterexample — baseline [1, 1, 3, 3], cylindrical [invalid, invalid, 2.4, 2.6] — to assert that the target is 3 on the paired cohort, not 2). "Reaches the target" iff the superiority interval [Q_0.00625, Q_0.99375] has its upper endpoint below 0, **or** TOST equivalence within ±3 % holds with each one-sided test at α = 0.0125, i.e. [Q_0.0125, Q_0.9875] strictly inside (−0.03, +0.03). The encoder-parameter ratio is reported for a pairing only if both its metric cells reach the target (2 777 984 vs 19 703 296 ≈ 7.1×; 19 750 912 vs 43 709 184 ≈ 2.2×). Every decision-driving interval is covered by the two-seed convergence gate. No other pairing is tested.
- **Cohorts (registered):** a plotted curve point, its seed SD and every fixed target use the arm's own cohort — the queries finite for all five seeds of that arm and metric; a comparison cell uses the paired cohort — queries finite for all five seeds in both arms of the cell. Both cohorts' sizes (queries and rooms) and the excluded queries per arm/seed/metric are reported; an empty cohort refuses the cell; where a plotted mean and a cell mean differ because of the cohorts, both are shown and labelled.
- **Descriptive:** the two curves (x = encoder parameters, log scale; y = error with seed SD and bootstrap intervals), the full-system parameter axis as a second panel, per-epoch test-loss trajectories, the yaw-robustness block per tier, throughput and memory per tier.

